//! ROADMAP SC-2 and SC-4, proven by ONE harness rather than two (plan 10-02).
//!
//! A ~100-event burst (D-01's locked scale) generated by FOUR concurrent real
//! `seam-client` subprocesses, landing in one receive loop and one timeline.
//! That combination proves the 200ms hook budget AND the arrival-ordering
//! guarantee in one place, and builds the expensive subprocess infrastructure
//! once.
//!
//! **Why this infrastructure is genuinely new.** Two existing things look like
//! they already do it and do not:
//!
//! - `tests/event_stream.rs::concurrent_senders_each_deliver_every_message_whole`
//!   (06-03) drives raw `UnixDatagram::unbound()` senders from THREADS inside
//!   one process. No real client binary is involved anywhere in it.
//! - `common::run_client` (07-04, via 10-01) is the only real-binary helper,
//!   and it blocks on `wait_with_output()` before returning. A loop over it is
//!   SEQUENTIAL by construction, and would pass against a server with no
//!   concurrency handling whatsoever.
//!
//! **What makes "concurrent" a fact here rather than a timing heuristic.**
//! `seam-client`'s `main` reads stdin to EOF (`read_to_string`) before it does
//! any work at all, so a child spawned with a PIPED BUT UNWRITTEN stdin is
//! blocked and alive. [`spawn_concurrent_clients`] returns every `Child` handle
//! having written nothing and waited on nothing; [`assert_all_alive`] then calls
//! `try_wait()` on each and requires `None` from all of them, which is a proof
//! that all four processes exist at the same instant. Only then does
//! [`write_all_stdins`] release them, all within microseconds of each other.
//!
//! **Delivery is MEASURED, never assumed.** `net.local.dgram.recvspace` is 4096
//! on this machine (measured three times across 06-01/06-02/06-03), so an
//! unpaced burst genuinely drops datagrams in the kernel -- 06-03 measured
//! 59.2%/60.2%/64.0% delivered across three runs of an unpaced 4x100 burst.
//! Every test below asserts the properties that survive load (no corruption, no
//! duplication, per-client order preserved, a floor on the total) and PRINTS the
//! real ratio. A test demanding 100/100 here is a test that flakes, and lowering
//! a flaky assertion later is how a phase quietly stops proving anything.
//!
//! Additive by construction: `tests/event_stream.rs` is Phase 6's regression net
//! and is not touched, and no production source changes in this plan.

